## Summary

I shipped a complete, native-idiomatic SwiftUI parity of the web `CronParser` devtools tool as **7 files** in `apps/apple/TeslaSync/feature-views/` (`CronParser.swift`, `.Model.swift`, `.Adapter.swift`, `.Views.swift`, `.Previews.swift`, `.Tests.swift`, `.strings`), modeled on the proven sibling surfaces Base64Tool (0011) and ColorConverter (0013).

**What it does (full parity, no stubs):** a faithful Swift port of `describeCron` + `getNextCronRuns` (incl. JS `parseInt`/`Number` quirks, `padStart`, weekday `0=Sun`, the year-of-minutes solver), the cron input with `timer` glyph, the 5 wrapping preset chips, the emerald description panel, and the numbered next-runs list. Every state renders (empty guidance, content, and a friendly no-upcoming-runs empty state — never a blank box), all chrome localized via the P1/S10 facade, bound through a P1/S8 `@Observable` model, token-driven (P1/S9), with `view.opened` telemetry (P1/S11) and VoiceOver labels.

**Verification:**
| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| swiftlint --strict (tree) | ✅ EXIT 0, 0 CronParser findings |
| swiftformat --lint (tree) | ✅ EXIT 0, 0/4062 need formatting |
| check-placeholders | ✅ COUNT 0 (literal + real scan of my files) |
